
  [1mThe fork that determines everything else.[0m Whether this is one person's task list or a shared one changes what
  "notification" even means — and it's the one decision that's expensive to reverse, because identity and a server leak
  into every layer below.

  - [1mSingle-user, one device[0m — "notification" means a reminder or an activity log. No identity, no server, no delivery
    infrastructure: tasks live in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, and a due-date reminder is a browser notification. Buys you a working
    app in a small, self-contained build. Costs you: "users" plural is off the table, and adding sharing later means
    rewriting storage and adding auth.
  - [1mSingle-user, multiple devices[0m — needs a server and storage, but no real identity model beyond "it's you." Middle
    ground; sync is the hard part, not notifications.
  - [1mMulti-user / shared team list[0m — the literal reading of your request. Needs a backend, accounts, auth, a subscription
    table, an event pipeline, and delivery that works when the recipient isn't looking at the page. This is a real
    application, several specs deep, and notifications is the final one.

  [1mMy recommendation: single-user, one device[0m, unless you know you need sharing. It gets a real working task app with
  genuine change notifications in one build, and you'll learn what changes actually matter to you before designing a
  subscription model around guesses. The cheap-to-change part is which events notify; the expensive part is the server
  and identity — so don't buy those until sharing is a requirement rather than a maybe.
